<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>This fictional modern episode addresses the presenting concerns below. [S]</paragraph><list><item>Breathlessness keeps him from walking any distance, and he uses a wheelchair at airports. [S]</item><item>Wants other smokers to quit before their lungs are damaged, and wants to keep working on his photography and writing. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Leonard Nimoy was born in Boston on March 26, 1931. [D]</item><item>He played Mr. Spock in the Star Trek television series from 1966 to 1969, later acted in and directed Star Trek films, and directed other films, including the comedy Three Men and a Baby. [D]</item><item>In a Twitter post at the end of January 2014 he announced that he had chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: &quot;I quit smoking 30 yrs ago. Not soon enough. I have COPD. Grandpa says, quit now!! LLAP&quot;. [D]</item><item>In early February 2014 he posted &quot;I&apos;m doing OK. Just can&apos;t walk distances,&quot; and ABC News reported that he had been seen at an airport in a wheelchair. [D]</item><item>In February 2014 he urged smokers to quit: &quot;Smokers, please understand. If you quit after you&apos;re diagnosed with lung damage it&apos;s too late.&quot; [D]</item></list><paragraph>3 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

